Mach-Number Degeneracy under Shock Re-acceleration of Fossil Electrons: 

A Theoretical Framework for Odd Radio Circle Rings

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

24 October 2026

Abstract

Odd radio circles (ORCs) are diffuse ring-like radio sources whose formation mechanism remains unresolved. Bruno et al. (2026) report an integrated spectral index $\alpha_{\rm int}=1.06\pm0.03$, a radial flattening to $\alpha\sim0.5$ at the ring of ORC J1555+2726 (ORC4), and a negative (convex) spectral curvature $\mathrm{SC}\sim-0.2$ between 75 and 160 kpc. They interpret these properties as evidence for shock re-acceleration of a fossil electron reservoir, caution against a literal strong-shock reading of the indices, and flag in their §5.3 that the 143–1260 MHz band may trace different re-acceleration and evolutionary stages. This chapter develops the test-particle re-acceleration framework required to interpret such spectra. Under pure re-acceleration, the asymptotic output index is the flatter of the seed index and the shock index, so a ring index near $0.5$ does not robustly determine the Mach number. The test-particle shock-index inversion $M^2=(2\alpha_{\rm sh}+3)/(2\alpha_{\rm sh}-1)$ is a relation between compression and asymptotic slope; it applies to a re-accelerated population when the observed slope equals the shock slope, which occurs asymptotically only on the steep-seed branch. The further step to an integrated-index estimate requires the cooling-offset approximation $\alpha_{\rm int}\approx\alpha_{\rm inj}+0.5$, which is an additional and non-trivial assumption for an expanding ORC shell. We prove two propositions and state one corollary with explicit restrictions: (i) an asymptotic min rule for re-accelerated power-law seeds (Proposition 1); (ii) a mixture-curvature identity stating that the logarithmic derivative of the local spectral index of any positive power-law superposition equals minus the weighted variance of the component indices (Proposition 2); and (iii) a curvature bound restricting the steepest uncooled, co-spatial, shock-front re-accelerated component to the shock index (Corollary 1). Applied to ORC4, the integrated index yields a finite effective Mach number only conditional on the cooling offset and on the flux scale. The 143 MHz flux scale is a model-selection systematic: at the lowest alternative value the inferred $\alpha_{\rm inj}$ falls below the test-particle DSA limit, which diagnoses a failure of the cooling-offset model rather than a physical impossibility. The ring index is the only input whose inversion formally diverges, and that divergence is a boundary artefact. Curvature is compatible with a mixture; the empirical control of Bruno et al. (Appendix C) is the primary argument for mixing, and the variance estimate is illustrative. The asymptotic amplification factor shows that steep-seed re-acceleration can substantially increase the high-momentum distribution relative to an unaccelerated seed, but a finite-spectrum emissivity calculation is required before the power budget can rank the branches. We state the specific conditions under which the framework's applicability to ORC4 would be weakened or rejected, distinguishing failure of the mathematical result from failure of its astrophysical interpretation.

---

1. Introduction

Odd radio circles are diffuse, edge-brightened ring sources of radius $\sim100$–$300$ kpc surrounding massive quiescent elliptical galaxies (Norris et al. 2021a, 2021b, 2022; Koribalski et al. 2021, 2024a, 2024b). Their integrated spectra are steep, $\alpha\sim1$–$1.5$, and their rings can be polarised at 20–30% with tangential magnetic fields (Norris et al. 2022; Taziaux et al. 2025). A shock is plausibly involved, but the acceleration mechanism is debated.

Bruno et al. (2026) report new LOFAR, uGMRT, and MeerKAT data for ORC4, with:

· integrated index $\alpha_{\rm int}=1.06\pm0.03$ at 50–1284 MHz;
· radial flattening to $\alpha\sim0.5$ at the ring radius ($\sim200$ kpc);
· negative spectral curvature $\mathrm{SC}\sim-0.2$ between 75 and 160 kpc;
· a colour–colour path that cannot be reproduced by thermal DSA plus ageing (their Appendix C).

They interpret these properties as evidence for re-acceleration of a fossil reservoir, with high-energy re-accelerated electrons dominating the ring at high frequency and low-energy re-accelerated electrons filling the volume. Their §5.3 cautions against a literal strong-shock reading of the indices, and flags that the 143–1260 MHz band may trace different re-acceleration and evolutionary stages.

The re-acceleration alternative is not derived in Bruno et al. (2026). This chapter develops it. §2 fixes notation and conventions. §3 states the domain of validity of the standard DSA inversion and separates the shock-index relation from the cooling-offset approximation. §4 proves the asymptotic min rule. §5 proves the mixture-curvature identity. §6 applies the results to ORC4 with explicit attention to systematics. §7 lists the measurements that would distinguish scenarios and the weaknesses of each test. §8 lists limitations and the conditions for failure.

