2. Physical setup and conventions

2.1 Shock and compression

Consider a planar, steady shock with upstream Mach number $M$. For an ideal gas with adiabatic index $\gamma=5/3$, the compression ratio is

r=\frac{4M^2}{M^2+3}.
\tag{1}

The downstream-to-upstream temperature ratio is

\frac{T_2}{T_1}=\frac{(5M^2-1)(M^2+3)}{16M^2}.
\tag{2}

Equation (2) is recorded for completeness and is not used in the derivations that follow.

2.2 Electron distribution and index convention

Throughout this chapter, $f(p)$ denotes the isotropic phase-space distribution function (particles per unit volume per unit momentum volume, averaged over solid angle), not the differential number distribution $n(p)=4\pi p^2 f(p)$. For a power-law phase-space distribution $f(p)\propto p^{-s}$ well above any synchrotron break, the synchrotron radio index is

\alpha=\frac{s-3}{2}.
\tag{3}

This convention is used consistently in Eq. (9) and in the synchrotron index relation Eq. (5). A differential number distribution would use a different index mapping: since $n(p)\propto p^2 f(p)$, the momentum slope shifts by $2$ (and the resulting $\alpha$ by $1$), so mixing conventions would propagate a systematic offset.

2.3 Diffusive shock acceleration

In the test-particle limit, the downstream phase-space distribution of accelerated particles is a power law,

f_{\rm DSA}(p)\propto p^{-q},\qquad q=\frac{3r}{r-1},
\tag{4}

with radio index

\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)=\frac{q-3}{2}=\frac{M^2+3}{2(M^2-1)}.
\tag{5}

Equation (5) diverges as $M\to1^+$ and tends to $0.5$ as $M\to\infty$.

2.4 Assumptions

The derivations in §§3–5 rest on:

A1 (test-particle). Accelerated electrons do not modify the shock structure.

A2 (steady, planar shock). The shock is old enough that the acceleration time is short compared with the dynamical time at the observed energies.

A3 (pure re-acceleration). The downstream distribution is the standard steady-state test-particle solution of the transport equation, with the upstream seed as the source term. No thermal injection term is included.

A4 (power-law seed). The upstream phase-space distribution is a power law $f_0=A\,p^{-s}$ over the relevant momentum range.

A5 (well-resolved momenta). The observed radio band probes momenta well above the seed's low-momentum end and below any cutoff.

Assumption A3 is the most restrictive. Its relaxation is discussed in §4.5.

