4. Pure re-acceleration: the asymptotic min rule

4.1 The re-acceleration integral

Under A1–A5, the downstream distribution is the standard steady-state test-particle solution (Drury 1983; Kang & Ryu 2011):

f_2(p)=q\,p^{-q}\int_{p_0}^{p}p'^{\,q-1}f_0(p')\,dp'.
\tag{9}

Equation (9) is the standard steady-state test-particle re-acceleration integral, which transforms the upstream seed distribution through a momentum-dependent shock kernel.

4.2 Closed form for a power-law seed

For $f_0=A\,p^{-s}$:

f_2(p)=\frac{qA}{q-s}\,p^{-s}\left[1-\left(\frac{p_0}{p}\right)^{q-s}\right],\qquad s\neq q,
\tag{10a}

f_2(p)=qA\,p^{-q}\ln\frac{p}{p_0},\qquad s=q.
\tag{10b}

4.3 Proposition 1 (asymptotic min rule)

Proposition 1. Let $f_0(p)=A\,p^{-s}$ be a power-law phase-space seed and let $f_2(p)$ be given by Eq. (9). Then, for $p\gg p_0$,

f_2(p)\propto p^{-\min(s,q)}\qquad\text{up to a logarithmic correction when }s=q,
\tag{11}

and in radio terms

\alpha_{\rm ring}\to\min\!\big(\alpha_{\rm seed},\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)\big).
\tag{12}

Proof.

Flat seed ($s<q$). In Eq. (10a), $q-s>0$, so $(p_0/p)^{q-s}\to0$. The bracket tends to $1$, and $f_2\propto p^{-s}$.

Steep seed ($s>q$). In Eq. (10a), $q-s<0$, so $(p_0/p)^{q-s}=(p/p_0)^{s-q}\to\infty$. The bracket $[1-(p/p_0)^{s-q}]$ diverges as $-(p/p_0)^{s-q}$. The prefactor $qA/(q-s)$ is negative. Combining, $f_2\propto p^{-s}\cdot(p/p_0)^{s-q}\propto p^{-q}$ up to a constant. The slope is $q$.

Marginal case ($s=q$). Equation (10b) gives $f_2\propto p^{-q}\ln(p/p_0)$, whose local logarithmic slope is $-q+1/\ln(p/p_0)$, flatter than $q$ by $1/\ln(p/p_0)$. The asymptotic slope is $q$, approached logarithmically slowly. $\square$

4.4 Physical consequences

Flat seed ($\alpha_{\rm seed}<\alpha_{\rm sh}$). The ring index tracks the seed and is independent of $M$. The amplitude is boosted by $q/(q-s)$.

Steep seed ($\alpha_{\rm seed}>\alpha_{\rm sh}$). The ring index tracks the shock. The amplitude depends on the seed's low-momentum moment, not on thermal injection efficiency.

Marginal case. The output index lies between the two values; convergence is logarithmic.

4.5 The thermal-injection term

Adding a thermal injection term $\propto p^{-q}$ to Eq. (9) does not break the min rule. Both the re-accelerated and injected components have asymptotic slope $q$ when $s>q$; they differ only in normalisation. When $s<q$, the flat-seed component dominates at high $p$ and the injected component dominates at lower $p$, with a crossover momentum $p_{\rm cross}$ set by the relative normalisations. The observed band must sit above $p_{\rm cross}$ for the flat-seed branch to apply cleanly. This is a condition on whether the 143–1260 MHz band sits above the crossover, not a failure of the min rule itself.

4.6 The power budget: a possible mechanism, not a solved budget

The three-orders-of-magnitude power deficit of thermal DSA in ORC4 (Bruno et al. 2026, §5.2) motivates considering whether re-acceleration can close it. The asymptotic amplification factors in the two branches are not symmetric.

Steep-seed branch ($s>q$). At momentum $p$ the asymptotic amplification factor is approximately $|q/(s-q)|\,(p/p_0)^{s-q}$, which grows without bound as $p/p_0$ increases. For $s-q=1$ and a representative lever arm $p/p_0=10^3$, the factor is $\sim q\cdot10^3$. The numerical choice $p/p_0=10^3$ is illustrative: it is a representative momentum lever arm, not a quantity derived from the data.

Flat-seed branch ($s<q$). The asymptotic amplification factor is $q/(q-s)$, which is order unity unless $s\approx q$. For a flat fossil seed at $\alpha_{\rm seed}=0.5$ ($s=4$) and a shock with $\alpha_{\rm sh}=0.75$ ($q=4.5$), the factor is $4.5/0.5=9$.

The asymptotic amplification factor shows that steep-seed re-acceleration can produce a large increase in the high-momentum distribution relative to the unaccelerated seed. It does not, by itself, demonstrate that the observed radio-power deficit is closed. Demonstrating closure requires a finite fossil spectrum with specified normalisation and cutoff, a magnetic-field model, radiative losses, and integration of the synchrotron emissivity over the emitting electron population. The present calculation therefore establishes a possible amplification mechanism, not a quantitative power-budget solution. Branch A (steep seed) remains a candidate on power grounds, but its quantitative advantage over Branch B is not established by the asymptotic ratio alone.

