6. Application to ORC4

6.1 Inputs

Quantity Value Source
$\alpha_{\rm int}$ (50–1284 MHz) $1.06\pm0.03$ (statistical) Bruno et al., §4.3
$\alpha_{\rm ring}$ (143–1260 MHz, 6″) $\sim0.5$ (no quoted error) Bruno et al., §4.4
$\alpha_{\rm inj}$ (675–1260 MHz) $0.7\pm0.2$ Bruno et al., §5.3
$\mathrm{SC}$ (75–160 kpc) $\sim-0.2$ Bruno et al., §4.4
Ring radius $\sim200$ kpc Bruno et al., §2

The ring index is quoted without an error bar in Bruno et al. (2026). In what follows, $\delta$ denotes an assumed effective uncertainty on $\alpha_{\rm ring}$, not a measured one.

6.2 The integrated index yields a conditional effective Mach estimate

Applying Eq. (7) to $\alpha_{\rm int}=1.06\pm0.03$ gives Eq. (8): $M\approx5.9$, finite under the cooling-offset approximation Eq. (7a).

6.2.1 A model-selection systematic on the integrated index

The $1.06\pm0.03$ is a bootstrap statistical error on the fluxes of Table 2 of Bruno et al. (2026). It does not include the flux-scale systematic, which is large at 143 MHz. Bruno et al. report four independent measurements of the 143 MHz flux: $74\pm7$ mJy (this work), $54\pm1$ mJy (De Gasperin et al. 2026), $39\pm10$ mJy (Norris et al. 2021b), and $97\pm10$ mJy (public LOFAR image).

To first order in a small shift $\Delta S$ at 143 MHz, the change in a seven-point fitted index is

\Delta\alpha\approx-\ln\!\left(1+\frac{\Delta S}{S_{143}}\right)\cdot\frac{\ln\nu_{143}-\langle\ln\nu\rangle}{\sum_j(\ln\nu_j-\langle\ln\nu\rangle)^2}.
\tag{8a}

With $\ln(143)-\langle\ln\nu\rangle\approx-1.11$ and $\sum_j(\ln\nu_j-\langle\ln\nu\rangle)^2\approx8.93$, Eq. (8a) becomes $\Delta\alpha\approx0.12\ln(S'_{143}/74)$. Evaluating at the four flux values:

$S_{143}'$ (mJy) $\alpha_{\rm int}$ $\alpha_{\rm inj}=\alpha_{\rm int}-0.5$ $M$ from Eq. (7)
39 (Norris) 0.98 0.48 undefined ($\alpha<1$)
54 (De Gasperin) 1.02 0.52 $\sim10$
74 (paper) 1.06 0.56 5.9
97 (public LOFAR) 1.09 0.59 4.8

Three features of this table require careful reading.

Hypersensitivity near the pole. The $54$ mJy case gives $M\sim10$ only because $\alpha_{\rm int}=1.02$ lies very close to the pole at $\alpha_{\rm int}=1$. The inversion Eq. (7) is hypersensitive near this pole: a change of $0.01$ in $\alpha_{\rm int}$ near $1.02$ changes $M$ by several units. Even the central value $\alpha_{\rm int}=1.06$ is only $\sim2\sigma$ from the pole ($1.06-1.00=0.06$, and $\sigma=0.03$), so the integrated-index Mach estimate is intrinsically fragile regardless of flux scale.

Direction of the 97 mJy case. A larger 143 MHz flux makes the seven-point fitted spectrum steeper, increasing $\alpha_{\rm int}$. The inferred $M$ nonetheless decreases, because the inversion $M^2=(\alpha_{\rm int}+1)/(\alpha_{\rm int}-1)$ decreases with increasing $\alpha_{\rm int}$ for $\alpha_{\rm int}>1$, and the inversion becomes singular as $\alpha_{\rm int}\to1^+$. No normalisation argument is required: the direction of the change in $M$ is fixed by the pole structure of Eq. (7).

The 39 mJy case. This gives $\alpha_{\rm inj}\approx0.48<0.5$. This does not show that the physical shock interpretation is impossible. It shows that the chain of assumptions leading from the integrated slope to an asymptotic test-particle DSA index—specifically the cooling-offset approximation Eq. (7a)—is inconsistent with that value under the assumed model. The measured integrated spectrum could reflect mixtures, curvature, calibration differences, or a failure of the steady continuous-injection cooling offset.

The 143 MHz flux scale is therefore not a statistical uncertainty on $M$. It is a model-selection systematic: the range of admissible $S_{143}$ spans the regime in which the cooling-offset DSA interpretation returns a finite $M$ (rows 54, 74, 97) and the regime in which that interpretation is inconsistent with an asymptotic test-particle DSA index (row 39). The systematic is at least as large as the formal statistical interval.

The same concern applies to the 407 MHz flux, which Bruno et al. correct upward by a factor $1.25\pm$ (empirically derived, §3.1). A 10% error on that correction shifts $\alpha_{143}^{675}$ and $\alpha_{675}^{1260}$ and therefore shifts $\mathrm{SC}$ by an amount comparable to the illustrative $\Delta a\sim0.86$ estimate of §5.3. The $\mathrm{SC}$ estimate should be read with that in mind.

Conclusion of §6.2. The integrated index does not robustly determine $M$. It returns a finite effective value under the cooling-offset approximation Eq. (7a), but the value depends on the flux scale and is hypersensitive near the pole at $\alpha_{\rm int}=1$, and at the lowest alternative flux scale the inferred $\alpha_{\rm inj}$ is inconsistent with interpreting that value as the asymptotic test-particle DSA index under the assumed cooling-offset model.

6.3 The ring index gives only a lower bound

On the steep-seed branch, $\alpha_{\rm ring}=0.5+\delta$ with Eq. (6) gives

M^2\ge\frac{4+2\delta}{2\delta}.
\tag{21}

$\delta$ 0.03 0.05 0.10 0.15 0.20
$M_{\min}$ 8.2 6.4 4.6 3.8 3.3

The table, not a single number, is the actual result. The value of $\delta$ is an assumed effective uncertainty, not a measured one; Bruno et al. (2026) quote no error on $\alpha_{\rm ring}$.

The 675–1260 MHz injection index, $0.7\pm0.2$, spans $M$ from $\sim2.5$ to infinity at $1\sigma$, with $3.3$ at the central value. The ring index supports neither an extremely strong shock nor a weak one on its own.

6.4 The curvature bound: a corollary of the min rule, restricted to the shock front

Corollary 1 (shock-front curvature bound). Under the min rule, a re-accelerated component that is (a) uncooled, (b) co-spatial with the flat component, and (c) at the shock front has index $\le\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)$. A component at index $1.36$ under these conditions requires $\alpha_{\rm sh}\ge1.36$, hence

M\lesssim1.82.
\tag{22}

Corollary 1 is not a new result; it is a direct consequence of Proposition 1. It is conditional on co-spatiality, no cooling, and shock-front location. It does not apply to:

· an azimuthally averaged $\mathrm{SC}$ measured between 75 and 160 kpc, which combines regions at different radii, ages, and shock stages;
· a "steep component" inferred from the variance identity, which is a mixture of populations, not a single component;
· a downstream index of $\alpha\sim1.2$–$1.5$, which Bruno et al. (§4.4) locate inside $\sim75$ kpc, not at the shock front, and which cooling can produce even downstream of a strong shock.

Bruno et al. (§5.2) are explicit that low-energy re-accelerated electrons are diffuse across the volume and dominate at low frequency, while high-energy electrons dominate the ring at high frequency and fade downstream due to radiative losses. The steep indices and the flat ring index are therefore not components of a single shock at a single time. Corollary 1 does not constrain the integrated $\mathrm{SC}$.

A downstream cooled population from a strong shock can have $\alpha=1.36$. Ageing models produce exactly such indices (Kardashev 1962; Pacholczyk 1970; Jaffe & Perola 1973). A finite fossil cloud, as in Kang's re-acceleration models for relics, also steepens the integrated spectrum beyond cooling alone.

6.5 The instability of the "flattest component" assumption

Proposition 2 gives $\Delta a\gtrsim0.86$ illustratively, but does not say what the flattest component is. If $\alpha_{\rm flat}=0.5$, the steep component is at $1.36$. If $\alpha_{\rm flat}=0.3$—allowed by the min rule and plausible for a fossil population previously accelerated by a strong shock—the steep component is at $1.16$, and the (already inapplicable) bound of Eq. (22) would become

\alpha_{\rm sh}\ge1.16\;\Rightarrow\;M\lesssim2.0.
\tag{22a}

The bound is unstable to an unmeasured input. It should not be used to constrain $M$.

6.6 The allowed region in $(M,\alpha_{\rm seed})$

Combining the min rule and the conditional integrated-index estimate, the allowed region is the union of three branches.

Branch A (strong shock, steep seed). On the steep-seed branch of the min rule, Eq. (21) gives $M_{\min}$ for each assumed $\delta$. If one takes $\delta=0.10$ as a representative effective uncertainty on the ring index, $M\gtrsim4.6$; if $\delta=0.05$, $M\gtrsim6.4$; if $\delta=0.20$, $M\gtrsim3.3$. The value $M\gtrsim4.6$ is conditional on $\delta=0.10$ and should be stated as such. On this branch, the seed is steeper than the shock, the output index is the shock index, and the asymptotic amplification factor can in principle be large (§4.6). Curvature is set by un-shocked, cooled, or weakly-shocked material, not by re-accelerated electrons.

Branch B (weak shock, broad seed). The seed has $\alpha_{\rm seed}\lesssim0.5$, so the flat-seed branch of the min rule gives $\alpha_{\rm ring}=\alpha_{\rm seed}\approx0.5$. The condition $\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)\ge\alpha_{\rm seed}$ is satisfied for any $M>1$. The min rule places no upper bound on $M$ on the flat-seed branch. The observed curvature is produced by the broadness of the seed itself, not by any single shock-accelerated component, so Corollary 1 does not apply. The asymptotic amplification factor is order unity unless $s\approx q$. Branch B is not excluded.

Branch C (distribution of $M$). $M$ spans a broad range across the shock surface, and the observed curvature and index mixture reflect the Mach spread. Consistent with the ORC simulations of Dolag et al. (2023) and Ivleva et al. (2026), and with the radio–X-ray Mach discrepancy in relics (Wittor et al. 2021). The power budget is intermediate: the steeper parts of the Mach distribution contribute the steep-seed amplification, while the flatter parts contribute seed-like spectra.

Classification. The integrated-index inversion (8) provides a conditional effective Mach estimate under the continuous-injection cooling-offset assumption Eq. (7a). It does not independently classify the source as Branch A, B, or C. Such a classification requires a spatially resolved forward model that jointly accounts for the ring, the diffuse emission, the seed spectrum, and radiative evolution. The ring-index lower bound (21) applies specifically to Branch A. The curvature data are compatible with all three. Curvature alone does not select a branch, and the min rule does not bound $M$ on the flat-seed branch. Power considerations motivate Branch A as a possible mechanism (§4.6), but a finite-spectrum emissivity calculation is required before they can rank the branches quantitatively.

