7.1 The index ahead of the ring is a weak test

Branch A requires a seed steeper than $\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)$. For $M\approx5$, $\alpha_{\rm sh}\approx0.58$, so Branch A requires $\alpha_{\rm seed}\gtrsim0.6$. Branch B requires $\alpha_{\rm seed}\lesssim0.5$. The two differ by $\sim0.1$ in seed index, which is at or below the typical measurement error on a deep low-frequency index. The seed-index test becomes useful only if the seed index can be measured to better than $\pm0.05$.

7.2 The 2–4 GHz test: a conditional consistency check

For a pure power-law mixture, Proposition 2 gives $d\alpha/d\ln\nu\le0$, so the 2–4 GHz index would be flatter than the 675–1260 MHz index.

It is not what the re-acceleration framework as a whole predicts, because re-accelerated populations cool, and cooling produces positive curvature. A steeper 2–4 GHz index would therefore falsify the pure power-law mixture reading of $\mathrm{SC}$, but be fully consistent with the re-acceleration framework once cooling is included, and not distinguish Branch A from Branch B.

A flatter 2–4 GHz index (say, near or below 0.5) would be consistent with mixing and inconsistent with a single aged power law, and would strengthen the mixture interpretation. The stacked VLASS image does not detect ORC4, so this test requires deeper high-frequency data.

---

8. Limitations and conditions for failure

The derivations rest on A1–A5 and hold in the test-particle, pure-reacceleration, power-law-seed limit.

Test-particle limit (A1). At high Mach number, accelerated particles modify the shock and $\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)$ is no longer given by Eq. (5). The lower bounds in §6.3 shift.

Pure re-acceleration (A3). If thermal injection coexists with re-acceleration, the min rule of Proposition 1 still applies at any $p$ where the re-accelerated component dominates; what changes is the crossover momentum. This is a condition, not a failure.

Asymptotic min rule. A young or finite-size shock has a maximum momentum; the observed band may sit in the transition between seed and shock slopes. The logarithmic correction at $s=q$ is slow.

Power-law seed (A4). Aged fossil plasma has a curved spectrum with a cutoff. Equation (9) still applies, but Proposition 2 holds only for power-law components.

Power budget. The asymptotic amplification factors of §4.6 show that steep-seed re-acceleration can produce a large increase in the high-momentum distribution, but they do not establish that the observed radio-power deficit is closed. That requires a finite-spectrum emissivity calculation with specified normalisation, cutoff, magnetic field, and radiative losses.

Projection. Line-of-sight overlap of ring and filling emission produces a mixture and negative curvature without re-acceleration, so $\mathrm{SC}<0$ does not by itself single out re-acceleration. The paper's simulation (Appendix C) argues against pure-thermal-DSA projection as the source, but projection combined with a fossil seed is not excluded.

Flattest component. Proposition 2 and Corollary 1 take the flattest component at $0.5$. A flat seed below $0.5$ is allowed by the min rule and lowers the implied steep-component index (§6.5).

Polarisation. The compression–polarisation link is model-dependent and has not been derived here.

Test-particle pressure. At high Mach number, accelerated-particle pressure can modify the shock structure. This does not automatically invalidate a high-$M$ solution, but the relevant parameter regime should be checked rather than assumed safe.

8.1 What would falsify the framework

The mathematical result in Proposition 1 is conditional on the steady test-particle re-acceleration model and its assumed seed distribution. Its applicability to ORC4 would be weakened or rejected by any of the following:

1. Failure of the asymptotic regime. A validated finite-age transport calculation shows that the observed radio-emitting momentum range does not approach the predicted minimum-slope behaviour for physically plausible ORC4 parameters.
2. Failure of the seed-dependence prediction. Under matched assumptions and a parameter regime where the model predicts asymptotic behaviour, a validated kinetic calculation finds that the output index systematically tracks the shock index independently of the seed slope.
3. Failure of the proposed ORC4 interpretation. A joint fit to the radio spectrum, spatially resolved spectral-index maps, curvature, morphology, and available independent constraints shows that physically plausible re-acceleration models cannot reproduce the observations, while an alternative model provides an adequate fit.

A steeper 2–4 GHz index alone does not falsify re-acceleration, because radiative cooling can steepen the high-frequency spectrum. Likewise, a successful single aged-spectrum fit would challenge the claim that a mixture is required to explain the data, but would not by itself disprove the possibility of fossil-electron re-acceleration.

8.2 Review status

This chapter has been through internal and referee-style review during drafting. It has not been journal peer-reviewed. Equation (9) is quoted as the standard re-acceleration result and has not been re-derived here. The numerical checks of Propositions 1 and 2 evaluate closed forms and are not independent solvers.

---

9. Conclusions

Solid results.

1. The test-particle shock-index inversion Eq. (6) is a relation between compression and asymptotic slope. It does not require thermal injection: it applies whenever the observed slope equals $\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)$, which occurs asymptotically on the steep-seed branch of re-acceleration. The additional step to an integrated-index estimate, Eq. (7), requires the cooling-offset approximation Eq. (7a), which is non-trivial for an expanding ORC shell.
2. The asymptotic min rule (Proposition 1) is proved: under pure re-acceleration, the output index is the flatter of seed and shock. A ring index near $0.5$ therefore does not robustly determine $M$. On the flat-seed branch, $M$ is unconstrained.
3. The mixture-curvature identity (Proposition 2) is proved: any positive superposition of fixed-index power laws has $d\alpha/d\ln\nu=-\operatorname{Var}_w\le0$. The empirical comparison with Bruno et al. (Appendix C) is the primary argument for a mixture, because it does not depend on the tent approximation; the variance estimate is illustrative only.

Conditional results.

4. The integrated index gives $M\approx5.9$ under the cooling-offset approximation Eq. (7a) and the 143 MHz flux scale of $74$ mJy, with unrounded $1\sigma$ endpoints $4.80$ and $8.23$. The flux scale is a model-selection systematic: at the Norris value $S_{143}\approx39$ mJy the inferred $\alpha_{\rm inj}$ falls below $0.5$, which is inconsistent with interpreting that value as the asymptotic test-particle DSA index under the assumed cooling-offset model. The inversion is also hypersensitive near the pole at $\alpha_{\rm int}=1$; the central value $1.06$ is only $\sim2\sigma$ from the pole. The integrated index does not robustly determine $M$.
5. The ring-index lower bounds in §6.3 are conditional on the steep-seed branch and on an assumed effective uncertainty $\delta$. The value $M\gtrsim4.6$ corresponds to $\delta=0.10$.
6. The asymptotic amplification factor of §4.6 shows that steep-seed re-acceleration can produce a large increase in the high-momentum distribution. It does not, by itself, demonstrate that the observed radio-power deficit is closed. A finite-spectrum emissivity calculation is required before power considerations can rank Branch A against Branch B.

Withdrawn or restricted claims.

7. The curvature bound $M\lesssim1.8$ of earlier versions is withdrawn for the integrated $\mathrm{SC}$ of ORC4. Corollary 1 survives only as a statement about co-spatial, uncooled, shock-front components, and it does not apply to the azimuthally averaged curvature measured between 75 and 160 kpc.
8. "Curvature is the sharpest constraint" is withdrawn. Curvature is compatible with all three branches; it does not select one.
9. The 2–4 GHz test is downgraded to a conditional consistency check: a flatter index is consistent with mixing, a steeper index is consistent with cooling, and neither falsifies the framework.
10. Branch B carries no $M\lesssim3$ bound. The min rule and Corollary 1 give no upper bound on $M$ on the flat-seed branch.
11. The claim that the integrated-index inversion "sits on Branch A or C" is withdrawn. The inversion provides a conditional effective Mach estimate under the cooling-offset approximation Eq. (7a); it does not independently classify the source. Such classification requires a spatially resolved forward model.

What the data actually support. The radio data of ORC4 are consistent with a broad distribution of Mach numbers (Branch C), and they do not require a single strong shock. The ring index alone supports neither a strong nor a weak shock. The integrated index, once systematics are included, does not robustly fix $M$; at the lowest alternative flux scale it is inconsistent with the cooling-offset interpretation of the test-particle DSA index, and the inversion is hypersensitive near the pole at $\alpha_{\rm int}=1$. The curvature requires a mixture or cooling, not a specific Mach number. The asymptotic amplification factor establishes steep-seed re-acceleration as a possible mechanism for closing the power deficit, but not as a demonstrated solution. The four measurements of §7 would help, but none is decisive on its own, and the seed-index test in particular is weak.

The framework is analytic, test-particle, and not yet journal peer-reviewed. Its central claim—that the ring index does not robustly determine $M$ under re-acceleration—is robust. Its quantitative corollaries are conditional, and §8 states the conditions under which they fail.

Appendices :

Appendix A. The steady-state re-acceleration integral

A.1 The transport equation

The isotropic, steady, one-dimensional diffusion–convection equation for the phase-space distribution $f(x,p)$ is

u\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial}{\partial x}\!\left(\kappa\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\right)=\frac{1}{3}\frac{du}{dx}\,p\,\frac{\partial f}{\partial p},
\tag{A.1}

with $u(x)$ the flow speed, $\kappa(x,p)$ the spatial diffusion coefficient, and $x$ increasing downstream. The right-hand side is the first-order Fermi operator in the small-angle-scattering limit. In the test-particle regime, the spectral index is related to the shock compression ratio $r=u_1/u_2$ by $q=3r/(r-1)$.

A.2 The downstream eigenfunction

Downstream ($x>0$) the flow is uniform, $u=u_2$, and $du/dx=0$. Equation (A.1) reduces to

u_2 f'-\kappa_2 f''=0,
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
f(x,p)=C_1(p)+C_2(p)\,e^{u_2x/\kappa_2}.
\tag{A.2}

Finiteness as $x\to+\infty$ requires $C_2=0$, so the downstream distribution is spatially uniform, $f=f_2(p)$. Its momentum dependence is fixed by the flux matching across the discontinuity at $x=0$.

A.3 The momentum-space jump condition

For monoenergetic injection at $p_1$ at the shock, the matching gives the standard Green's function

f_2(p;\,p_1)=q\,p_1^{-1}\left(\frac{p}{p_1}\right)^{-q}\Theta(p-p_1),
\qquad
q\equiv\frac{3u_1}{u_1-u_2}=\frac{3r}{r-1},
\tag{A.3}

with $\Theta$ the Heaviside step. The step encodes the fact that test-particle re-acceleration moves particles upward in momentum only. Because (A.1) is linear in $f$, the downstream distribution for a general seed is the superposition of (A.3) weighted by the seed:

f_2(p)=q\,p^{-q}\int_{p_0}^{p}p'^{\,q-1}f_0(p')\,dp',
\tag{A.4}

which is Eq. (9) of the main text. The lower limit $p_0$ is the low-momentum end of the seed; the upper limit is $p$ because of the step in (A.3). This integral is the standard steady-state test-particle re-acceleration solution.

A.4 Verification of the differential form

Differentiating (A.4) with respect to $p$,

p\,f_2'(p)+q\,f_2(p)=q\,f_0(p).
\tag{A.5}

Equation (A.5) is the compact statement of the jump condition: the re-accelerated downstream slope is the shock slope $q$ modified by the seed term $q f_0$. In the absence of a seed ($f_0=0$), the solution is the pure eigenfunction $f_2\propto p^{-q}$.

A.5 Scope of the derivation

The slope $q$ in (A.3) is independent of the momentum dependence of $\kappa$ provided $\kappa$ has the same momentum scaling upstream and downstream. The derivation assumes A1 (test-particle), A2 (steady), and a spatially thin shock; it does not assume thermal injection.

---

Appendix B. Closed forms and the local logarithmic slope

B.1 Evaluation of the integral

For $f_0=A\,p^{-s}$ and $s\neq q$, the integral in (A.4) is

f_2(p)=\frac{qA}{q-s}\,p^{-s}\left[1-\left(\frac{p_0}{p}\right)^{q-s}\right],
\tag{B.1}

which is Eq. (10a). For $s=q$ the integrand is $p'^{-1}$ and

f_2(p)=qA\,p^{-q}\ln\!\left(\frac{p}{p_0}\right),
\tag{B.2}

which is Eq. (10b). Both (B.1) and (B.2) satisfy $f_2(p_0)=0$, as required by the step in (A.3).

B.2 A master formula for the local index

Define the local logarithmic slope

s_{\rm loc}(p)\equiv-\frac{d\ln f_2}{d\ln p},
\qquad
\alpha_{\rm loc}=\frac{s_{\rm loc}-3}{2}.
\tag{B.3}

Substituting $p f_2'=-s_{\rm loc}f_2$ into (A.5) gives, for any seed,

\boxed{\;s_{\rm loc}(p)=q\left[1-\frac{f_0(p)}{f_2(p)}\right]\;}
\tag{B.4}

This is an exact consequence of (A.5) and requires no asymptotic limit. For $f_2>0$ and $f_0>0$, the bracket is strictly less than unity, so

s_{\rm loc}(p)<q\quad\text{for all }p>p_0,
\tag{B.5}

with equality only where $f_0(p)=0$ (a seed cutoff) or asymptotically. In radio terms, $\alpha_{\rm loc}(p)<\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)$ strictly. This strengthens Corollary 1: the bound does not require the asymptotic limit; it holds at every momentum at which the seed is non-zero, under A1–A5. The conditions on Corollary 1 (single power-law seed, pure re-acceleration, no cooling, single co-spatial component) are unchanged, and those conditions are what limit its application to the azimuthally averaged curvature of ORC4.

B.3 Explicit closed forms

Inserting (B.1) and (B.2) into (B.4), with $x\equiv p/p_0$:

Flat seed ($s<q$).
s_{\rm loc}(p)=s-\frac{q-s}{x^{\,q-s}-1},
\qquad
x>1,
\tag{B.6a}


which approaches $s$ from below as $x\to\infty$.

Steep seed ($s>q$).
s_{\rm loc}(p)=s-\frac{s-q}{1-x^{-(s-q)}},
\qquad
x>1,
\tag{B.6b}


which approaches $q$ from below as $x\to\infty$.

Marginal case ($s=q$).
s_{\rm loc}(p)=q-\frac{1}{\ln x},
\tag{B.6c}


which approaches $q$ logarithmically slowly.

The deviations from the asymptotic values in (B.6) scale as

|s_{\rm loc}-\min(s,q)|\sim|s-q|\,x^{-|s-q|},
\qquad x\gg1,
\tag{B.7}

for both branches: the approach is algebraic, and slow when $|s-q|\ll1$. This is the quantitative statement behind the "marginal case" caveat of §4.3.

