Appendix F. The cooling-offset relation and its domain of validity

F.1 Derivation

Let the injection spectrum be $Q(\gamma)=Q_0\gamma^{-p}$, continuous in time, and let the dominant loss be radiative, $d\gamma/dt=-\beta\gamma^2$ in the Thomson regime. The steady-state continuity equation in energy space,

\frac{\partial}{\partial\gamma}\!\left(\beta\gamma^2N\right)=-Q(\gamma),
\tag{F.1}

integrates to

\beta\gamma^2N(\gamma)=\int_\gamma^\infty Q(\gamma')\,d\gamma'=\frac{Q_0}{p-1}\gamma^{1-p},
\tag{F.2}

hence

N(\gamma)\propto\gamma^{-(p+1)}.
\tag{F.3}

The injection index gives $\alpha_{\rm inj}=(p-1)/2$, and the cooled index is

\alpha=\frac{(p+1)-1}{2}=\frac{p}{2}=\alpha_{\rm inj}+0.5,
\tag{F.4}

which is Eq. (7a). The offset of $0.5$ is exact for a steady, continuously injected population dominated by $\gamma^2$ losses above a cooling break. This is the standard relation used in relic analyses, $\alpha_{\rm int}=\alpha_{\rm inj}+0.5$, derived from the Kardashev (1962) continuous-injection solution.

F.2 Domain of validity

Equation (F.4) requires (i) continuous injection, (ii) a steady state at the observed $\gamma$, (iii) $\gamma^2$ losses in the Thomson regime, (iv) negligible adiabatic losses and negligible re-acceleration, and (v) an observed band lying wholly above the cooling break. For an ORC shell with a dynamical time comparable to the radiative age, conditions (ii) and (iv) are not established. If the observed band straddles the break, the effective offset lies between $0$ and $0.5$. Incomplete cooling can produce offsets different from the canonical $0.5$.

F.3 The offset is a first-order systematic on $M$

Because $\alpha_{\rm inj}=\alpha_{\rm int}-\Delta$ with $\Delta$ the offset, and $M$ is a steep function of $\alpha_{\rm inj}$ near $\alpha_{\rm inj}\to0.5$, the offset dominates the Mach estimate. Table F.1 evaluates Eq. (6) at $\alpha_{\rm int}=1.06$ for a range of assumed offsets.

