Appendix G. The window function for band curvature

G.1 Band-averaged index

Define the band-averaged index over $[\nu_1,\nu_2]$ as

\langle\alpha\rangle_{[\nu_1,\nu_2]}=\frac{\int_{\nu_1}^{\nu_2}\alpha(\nu)\,W(\nu)\,d\ln\nu}{\int_{\nu_1}^{\nu_2}W(\nu)\,d\ln\nu},
\tag{G.1}

with $W$ the flux weighting set by the map construction. For a pure power law, $\alpha$ is constant and $\langle\alpha\rangle=\alpha$ independently of $W$. For a mixture, $\langle\alpha\rangle$ is a weighted average of the local index over the band.

G.2 The tent approximation

Approximating each band average by the local index at the band's geometric mean $\bar\nu$, Eq. (18) becomes

\mathrm{SC}\simeq\alpha(\bar\nu_{c})-\alpha(\bar\nu_{a})
=\int_{\bar\nu_a}^{\bar\nu_c}\frac{d\alpha}{d\ln\nu}\,d\ln\nu,
\tag{G.2}

with $\bar\nu_a=\sqrt{143\cdot675}\approx311$ MHz and $\bar\nu_c=\sqrt{675\cdot1260}\approx922$ MHz. Substituting (14),

\mathrm{SC}\simeq-\int_{\bar\nu_a}^{\bar\nu_c}\operatorname{Var}_w(a)\,d\ln\nu
=-\langle\operatorname{Var}_w\rangle\ln\!\left(\frac{922}{311}\right),
\tag{G.3}

which is Eq. (19) with a top-hat window, the "tent" of §5.2. The tent area is

\ln\!\left(\frac{922}{311}\right)=1.087.
\tag{G.4}

For $\mathrm{SC}=-0.2$, Eq. (G.3) gives $\langle\operatorname{Var}_w\rangle=0.184$, and $\Delta a\ge2\sqrt{0.184}=0.857$, quoted as $0.86$ in Eq. (20).

G.3 Why the estimate is illustrative

Equation (G.3) fails to be a derivation for four reasons, each of which is a direction of systematic error rather than a statistical one.

1. Band averages are not point values. Replacing $\langle\alpha\rangle$ by $\alpha(\bar\nu)$ ignores the curvature of $\alpha(\nu)$ within each band, which is largest where $\operatorname{Var}_w$ is largest.
2. $\operatorname{Var}_w$ is not constant. Table D.1 shows it varying by two orders of magnitude across three decades for a two-component mixture. The inferred $\langle\operatorname{Var}_w\rangle$ is a weighted mean whose value depends on where the components cross over, which is not measured.
3. The map weighting $W$ is not the top hat. The observed $\mathrm{SC}$ is computed from maps with finite resolution, masking, and flux weighting; the effective window is not the top hat of (G.3).
4. The observed $\mathrm{SC}$ is spatially averaged. The 75–160 kpc average combines regions at different radii with different cooling histories, so the measured $\mathrm{SC}$ is not the curvature of a single population.

For these reasons the variance estimate is used in this chapter only as an order-of-magnitude guide (§5.2, §5.3). The primary argument for a mixture is the control comparison of §5.4.

