Appendix H. Exact error propagation through the pole of Eq. (7)

H.1 Exact inversion

From Eq. (7),

M(\alpha)=\left(\frac{\alpha+1}{\alpha-1}\right)^{1/2},
\qquad \alpha>1.
\tag{H.1}

Equation (H.1) has a simple pole at $\alpha=1$ and is monotonically decreasing on $(1,\infty)$.

H.2 Logarithmic derivative

\frac{d\ln M}{d\alpha}
=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{1}{\alpha+1}-\frac{1}{\alpha-1}\right)
=-\frac{1}{\alpha^2-1}.
\tag{H.2}

At $\alpha=1.06$, $\alpha^2-1=0.1236$ and $d\ln M/d\alpha=-8.09$; the linearised fractional error is therefore

\frac{\sigma_M}{M}=\frac{\sigma_\alpha}{\alpha^2-1}=8.09\times0.03=0.243,
\tag{H.3}

i.e. $\sigma_M=1.42$ on $M=5.86$. This underestimates the upper error because (H.1) is strongly curved.

H.3 Curvature of the inversion

\frac{d^2\ln M}{d\alpha^2}=\frac{2\alpha}{(\alpha^2-1)^2}=138.7
\quad\text{at }\alpha=1.06,
\tag{H.4}

so the second-order term is comparable to the first for $\sigma_\alpha=0.03$. The linearised error of (H.3) is not adequate, and the exact endpoint evaluation is required.

H.4 Exact endpoints

Evaluating (H.1) at the $1\sigma$ endpoints of $\alpha_{\rm int}$:

\alpha=1.03\Rightarrow M=8.225,
\qquad
\alpha=1.06\Rightarrow M=5.856,
\qquad
\alpha=1.09\Rightarrow M=4.819.
\tag{H.5}

The exact asymmetric errors are $+2.37$ and $-1.04$.

