Appendix I. Finite-cutoff seed: why the asymptotic amplification factor is not a power budget

I.1 The question

Equation (B.1) gives the ratio of the re-accelerated to the unaccelerated distribution at the same momentum. On the steep branch this ratio grows as $p^{\,s-q}$ and can be made arbitrarily large by evaluating it at sufficiently high $p$. Section 4.6 uses this ratio as a heuristic for whether re-acceleration can close the $10^3$ power deficit of thermal DSA in ORC4. This appendix shows quantitatively why the ratio cannot serve that purpose, and why neither branch is ranked by it.

I.2 A seed with a sharp cutoff

Take

f_0(p)=A\,p^{-s},\qquad p_0\le p\le p_c,
\tag{I.1}

and zero outside. For $s>q$ and $p>p_c$, Eq. (A.4) gives

f_2(p)=qA\,p^{-q}\int_{p_0}^{p_c}p'^{\,q-1-s}\,dp'
=\frac{qA}{s-q}\,p^{-q}\left(p_0^{-(s-q)}-p_c^{-(s-q)}\right).
\tag{I.2}

Equation (I.2) is a pure power law of slope $q$ with no cutoff: re-acceleration regenerates a high-energy tail beyond $p_c$, with a normalisation set by the seed's content below $p_c$. The ratio $f_2/f_0$ at $p>p_c$ therefore diverges as $p^{-q}/p^{-s}=p^{\,s-q}$ exactly because the seed at those momenta is vanishingly small. Fossil electron populations are indeed modelled with a power law and an exponential or sharp cutoff, with the re-accelerated spectrum depending on whether $q\le s$ (strong shocks) or $q>s$ (weak shocks).

I.3 The fractional uptake

The physically meaningful comparison is between the numbers of particles, not the values of $f$ at a single momentum. With $s-q=1$, $q=4.5$, and $R\equiv p_c/p_0=10^3$:

N_0=4\pi\!\int_{p_0}^{p_c}p^2f_0\,dp
=2\pi A\,p_0^{-2}\left(1-R^{-2}\right),
\tag{I.3}

N_2(>p_c)=4\pi\!\int_{p_c}^{\infty}p^2f_2\,dp
=\frac{8\pi qA}{1.5}\,p_0^{-2}\,R^{-1.5}\left(1-R^{-0.5}\right),
\tag{I.4}

so that

\frac{N_2(>p_c)}{N_0}
=\frac{4q\,R^{-1.5}\left(1-R^{-0.5}\right)}{1.5\left(1-R^{-2}\right)}
\approx3.7\times10^{-4}.
\tag{I.5}

Only $0.04\%$ of the seed's particles are re-accelerated above the cutoff, even though the ratio $f_2/f_0$ at $p=p_c$ is $\sim 1.4\times10^2$ and grows without bound above it. The large ratio and the small uptake are the same fact seen from two sides. The cutoff momentum $p_c$ in fossil-electron re-acceleration models is typically $p_c/m_ec\sim10^3$–$10^4$, so the uptake is small for reasonable choices.

I.4 The flat branch for comparison

On the flat branch ($s<q$) with the same cutoff, Eq. (B.1) gives

\frac{f_2(p)}{f_0(p)}=\frac{q}{q-s}\left[1-\left(\frac{p_0}{p}\right)^{q-s}\right]\le\frac{q}{q-s},
\tag{I.6}

uniformly in $p$ and of order $q/(q-s)$. For the example of §4.6 ($q=4.5$, $s=4$) the factor is $9$, and it applies to the bulk of the seed rather than to a negligible high-momentum tail. The flat branch therefore delivers a modest but genuine boost to the whole population, whereas the steep branch delivers a large boost to a negligible fraction of it.

I.5 Consequence for §4.6 and §6.6

The asymptotic amplification factor is not a power budget in either branch. Converting it into a synchrotron luminosity requires the absolute normalisation $A$, the cutoff $p_c$, the magnetic field, the emitting volume, and the radiative losses — precisely the inputs that §4.6 lists as absent. The heuristic of §4.6 should therefore be read as establishing a possible mechanism only.

This appendix sharpens the statement of §4.6 that Branch A's quantitative advantage is "not established by the asymptotic ratio alone," and it supports the wording of §6.6 that power considerations "motivate" Branch A as a possible mechanism. It does not support ranking Branch A above Branch B on power grounds. The ranking requires the finite-spectrum forward model identified in §4.6 and §9, item 6.

