Appendix J. Conventions, index conversions, and notation

J.1 Distribution functions

Three distribution functions appear in the literature, with different index conventions:

f(p)\quad\text{(phase space)},\qquad
n(p)=4\pi p^2f(p)\quad\text{(differential number)},\qquad
N(\gamma)\quad\text{(energy space)}.
\tag{J.1}

This chapter uses $f(p)$ throughout, as stated in §2.2. The conversions are:

f\propto p^{-s}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
n\propto p^{-(s-2)}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\alpha=\frac{s-3}{2}=\frac{(s-2)-1}{2}.
\tag{J.2}

Using $n(p)$ instead of $f(p)$ therefore shifts the momentum index by $2$ and the radio index by $1$. The "$4\pi$" in (J.1) is the solid-angle normalisation and does not affect any index.

J.2 The shock index

q=\frac{3u_1}{u_1-u_2}=\frac{3r}{r-1},
\qquad
r=\frac{u_1}{u_2}=\frac{(\gamma+1)M^2}{(\gamma-1)M^2+2}=\frac{4M^2}{M^2+3}
\quad(\gamma=5/3),
\tag{J.3}

\alpha_{\rm sh}(M)=\frac{q-3}{2}=\frac{M^2+3}{2(M^2-1)}.
\tag{J.4}

The Mach-number inversion is

M^2=\frac{2\alpha_{\rm sh}+3}{2\alpha_{\rm sh}-1},
\tag{J.5}

which is Eq. (6) of the main text and the standard test-particle DSA relation.

J.3 The symbol $\gamma$

The symbol $\gamma$ denotes the adiabatic index in §2.1 and Appendix J.2 ($\gamma=5/3$), and the electron Lorentz factor in Appendix F. The two usages never occur in the same equation. The adiabatic index is not used outside §2.1 and J.2.

